          "title": "Exam Questions (With Complete Answers)",
          "markdown": "## 5. Exam Questions (With Complete Answers)\n\n### Short Answer Questions (2 Marks Each)\n\n1. **Differentiate between ideal and practical voltage sources.**\n   **Answer:** An *ideal* voltage source has zero internal resistance ($R_{\\text{int}} = 0$) and maintains constant terminal voltage regardless of load current. A *practical* voltage source has small series resistance $R_s$, so terminal voltage drops as $V_L = V_s - I R_s$.\n2. **Define unilateral and bilateral elements with two examples each.**\n   **Answer:** *Bilateral elements* conduct identically in either direction ($R, L, C$). *Unilateral elements* have characteristics depending on current/polarity direction (p-n diode, BJT).\n3. **Why can't we apply KVL directly across a loop containing a current source?**\n   **Answer:** Because the voltage drop across an ideal current source is unknown and determined solely by the rest of the circuit; we instead eliminate that branch via a **supermesh** or fixed loop current.\n4. **State the physical conservation principles behind KCL and KVL.** *(BEU 2026)*\n   **Answer:** KCL reflects **conservation of electric charge** (charge cannot accumulate at a node); KVL reflects **conservation of energy** (net work done around any closed loop is zero).\n5. **Convert a balanced Star network of $R_Y = 3\\ \\Omega$ per arm into an equivalent Delta network.** *(BEU 2026)*\n   **Answer:** For equal Star arms, $R_\\Delta = 3 R_Y = 3 \\times 3 = \\mathbf{9\\ \\Omega}$ per side.\n\n### Long Answer Questions (7–10 Marks Each)\n\n1. **Explain the step-by-step procedure of Mesh Analysis (including Supermesh) and Nodal Analysis with labeled circuit diagrams.** *(Present Methods B & C + solve Problem 4 as worked illustration using the DC Circuits diagram above, part (b).)*\n2. **State and derive the formulas for Star-to-Delta ($\\text{Y} \\to \\Delta$) and Delta-to-Star ($\\Delta \\to \\text{Y}$) resistance transformations.** *(Equate equivalent resistance between pairs of terminals: $R_1 + R_2 = \\frac{R_{12}(R_{13} + R_{23})}{R_{12}+R_{23}+R_{13}}$, solve symmetrically to get $R_1 = \\frac{R_{12}R_{13}}{\\sum R}$ and $R_{12} = R_1 + R_2 + \\frac{R_1 R_2}{R_3}$.)*\n3. **Classify electrical circuit elements (active/passive, linear/nonlinear, unilateral/bilateral, lumped/distributed) and explain practical source transformations with examples.** *(Include $R, L, C$ time-domain laws, $V\\text{–}I$ curves, and the DC Circuits diagram above, part (a).)*"
