          "title": "Big Picture & Simple Concepts",
          "markdown": "![AC Sinusoid, Impedance/Power Triangles and 3-Phase Star/Delta](/notes/diagrams/u2-ac-phasor-power-3phase.svg)\n\n\n## 1. Big Picture & Simple Concepts\n\nWall outlets supply **Alternating Current (AC)** at $230\\text{ V}, 50\\text{ Hz}$ rather than flat DC because AC voltages can be stepped up and down effortlessly with transformers.\n1. **Sine Wave Numbers ($V_m, V_{\\text{avg}}, V_{\\text{rms}}$):** Since a sine wave flips positive and negative evenly, its full-cycle mean is zero—so **Average Value** is computed over a half-cycle ($0.637 V_m$), while **RMS Value** ($0.707 V_m$) measures the effective DC voltage that delivers the same heat in a resistor.\n2. **Phasors (Rotating Arrows):** Instead of integrating sines and cosines, we freeze a wave $v(t) = V_m\\sin(\\omega t + \\theta)$ as a complex arrow (**phasor**) $\\mathbf{V} = V_{\\text{rms}}\\angle\\theta$. Pure inductors lag current behind voltage by $90^\\circ$ (\"CIVIL\"), while pure capacitors lead current ahead of voltage by $90^\\circ$—and **neither consumes real power ($P_{\\text{avg}} = 0$)**!\n3. **Impedance ($Z$), Admittance ($Y$) & Power Triangle:** Total AC opposition is complex **Impedance** $Z = R + jX$ ($X = X_L - X_C$), whose inverse is **Admittance** $Y = G + jB$. Multiplying voltage and current gives **Apparent Power** $S = VI$ ($\\text{VA}$), split into useful **Real Power** $P = VI\\cos\\phi$ ($\\text{W}$) and sloshing **Reactive Power** $Q = VI\\sin\\phi$ ($\\text{VAR}$).\n4. **Three-Phase Systems:** Three equal sine waves spaced $120^\\circ$ apart deliver **constant non-pulsating torque** to industrial motors while needing less copper than single-phase feeders."
