          "title": "Big Picture & Simple Concepts",
          "markdown": "![Single-Phase Transformer, Equivalent Circuit, DC Machine and 3-Phase Induction Motor](/notes/diagrams/u3-transformer-dc-induction.svg)\n\n\n## 1. Big Picture & Simple Concepts\n\nElectrical machines convert energy between electrical form and mechanical rotation (or between two AC voltage levels) using magnetic fields:\n1. **Electromagnetic Laws:** Moving a wire through a magnet—or changing current in a coil—induces EMF (**Faraday's Law**), always opposing the change that made it (**Lenz's Law**). That creates **self-inductance $L$** (opposition to own current change) and **mutual inductance $M$** (transferring power wirelessly between two coils!).\n2. **Transformers (Static AC Machines):** Two coils wrapped on a laminated silicon-steel core step AC voltage up ($11\\text{ kV} \\to 400\\text{ kV}$ to slash line losses) or down ($230\\text{ V} \\to 12\\text{ V}$ for chargers) with $97\\text{–}99\\%$ efficiency and no moving parts!\n3. **DC Machines:** Have a spinning **armature** + **commutator** (mechanical rectifier) + stationary field poles. Run mechanically to make DC (**Generator**), or feed DC to spin (**Motor**: **Series** for high starting torque in cranes/traction; **Shunt** for constant-speed lathes/conveyors).\n4. **Induction Motors (AC Workhorses):** No commutator! 3-phase stator currents create a **rotating magnetic field (RMF)** at synchronous speed $N_s = 120f/P$, dragging the rotor slightly slower ($N_r < N_s$, **slip $s$**). Single-phase induction motors (in fans, mixers, washing machines—called **fractional horsepower motors**) need an auxiliary start winding + capacitor because one phase alone cannot start spinning on its own!"
