          "id": "ch-4-solved-numericals-8-machines-problems",
          "number": "4",
          "title": "Solved Numericals (8 Machines Problems)",
          "markdown": "## 4. Solved Numericals (8 Machines Problems)\n\n### Problem 1 (Self-Inductance from Power Flow)\nFind the inductance $L$ of a coil in which a current of $i = 0.2\\text{ A}$ increasing at $\\frac{di}{dt} = 0.4\\text{ A/s}$ represents a power flow of $0.4\\text{ W}$.\n\n**Solution:**\n1. Instantaneous power $P = v \\cdot i \\implies v = \\frac{P}{i} = \\frac{0.4\\text{ W}}{0.2\\text{ A}} = \\mathbf{2.0\\text{ V}}$.\n2. Since $v = L\\frac{di}{dt}$:\n   $$L = \\frac{v}{di/dt} = \\frac{2.0}{0.4} = \\mathbf{5.0\\text{ H}}$$\n\n---\n\n### Problem 2 (Mutual Inductance & Induced Secondary EMF)\nTwo coils have $L_1 = 0.4\\text{ H}$, $L_2 = 0.9\\text{ H}$, and coupling coefficient $k = 0.8$. Find mutual inductance $M$, and the EMF induced in coil 2 if current in coil 1 changes at $50\\text{ A/s}$.\n\n**Solution:**\n1. $M = k\\sqrt{L_1 L_2} = 0.8\\sqrt{0.4 \\times 0.9} = 0.8\\sqrt{0.36} = 0.8 \\times 0.6 = \\mathbf{0.48\\text{ H}}$.\n2. $|e_2| = M\\frac{di_1}{dt} = 0.48 \\times 50 = \\mathbf{24.0\\text{ V}}$.\n\n---\n\n### Problem 3 (Transformer Turns & Core Peak Flux)\nA $2200/220\\text{ V}, 50\\text{ Hz}$ single-phase transformer has $N_2 = 40$ secondary turns. Calculate primary turns $N_1$ and maximum core flux $\\Phi_m$.\n\n**Solution:**\n1. Turns ratio: $\\frac{N_1}{N_2} = \\frac{E_1}{E_2} = \\frac{2200}{220} = 10 \\implies N_1 = 10 \\times 40 = \\mathbf{400\\text{ turns}}$.\n2. From $E_2 = 4.44 f N_2 \\Phi_m$:\n   $$\\Phi_m = \\frac{220}{4.44 \\times 50 \\times 40} = \\frac{220}{8880} = 0.024775\\text{ Wb} = \\mathbf{24.78\\text{ mWb}}$$\n\n---\n\n### Problem 4 (Transformer Efficiency & Max Efficiency Load)\nA $100\\text{ kVA}$ single-phase transformer has iron loss $P_i = 600\\text{ W}$ and full-load copper loss $P_{cu,\\text{fl}} = 1.5\\text{ kW} = 1500\\text{ W}$. Calculate efficiency at: (i) full load, $0.8$ lagging pf, (ii) half load ($x=0.5$), unity pf, and (iii) kVA at maximum efficiency.\n\n**Solution:**\n- **(i) Full load ($x=1, \\cos\\phi=0.8$):** Output $= 100{,}000 \\times 0.8 = 80{,}000\\text{ W}$.\n  $$\\eta_{\\text{FL}} = \\frac{80{,}000}{80{,}000 + 600 + 1500}\\times 100\\% = \\frac{80{,}000}{82{,}100}\\times 100\\% = \\mathbf{97.44\\%}$$\n- **(ii) Half load ($x=0.5, \\cos\\phi=1.0$):** Output $= 0.5 \\times 100{,}000 \\times 1 = 50{,}000\\text{ W}$; $P_{cu} = (0.5)^2 \\times 1500 = 375\\text{ W}$.\n  $$\\eta_{\\text{HL}} = \\frac{50{,}000}{50{,}000 + 600 + 375}\\times 100\\% = \\frac{50{,}000}{50{,}975}\\times 100\\% = \\mathbf{98.09\\%}$$\n- **(iii) Load for max efficiency:** $S_{\\max\\eta} = 100\\sqrt{\\frac{600}{1500}} = 100\\sqrt{0.4} = \\mathbf{63.25\\text{ kVA}}$.\n\n---\n\n### Problem 5 (Equivalent Impedance & Voltage Regulation)\nA $20\\text{ kVA}, 2000\\text{ V}/200\\text{ V}, 50\\text{ Hz}$ transformer has $R_1 = 1.5\\ \\Omega, X_1 = 2\\ \\Omega$, $R_2 = 0.015\\ \\Omega, X_2 = 0.02\\ \\Omega$. Determine: (i) $R_{01}, X_{01}, Z_{01}$ referred to primary, (ii) full-load copper loss, and (iii) $\\%$ voltage regulation at full load, $0.8$ pf lagging.\n\n**Solution:**\n1. Turns ratio $a = N_1/N_2 = 2000/200 = 10$ ($a^2 = 100$).\n   $$R_{01} = 1.5 + 100(0.015) = \\mathbf{3.0\\ \\Omega}, \\quad X_{01} = 2.0 + 100(0.02) = \\mathbf{4.0\\ \\Omega}, \\quad Z_{01} = \\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = \\mathbf{5.0\\ \\Omega}$$\n2. Full-load primary current $I_1 = \\frac{20{,}000}{2000} = 10\\text{ A}$. Full-load copper loss:\n   $$P_{cu} = I_1^2 R_{01} = 10^2 \\times 3.0 = \\mathbf{300\\text{ W}}$$\n3. Voltage regulation at $0.8$ lagging ($\\cos\\phi = 0.8, \\sin\\phi = 0.6$):\n   $$\\%\\text{Reg} = \\frac{10(3.0\\times 0.8 + 4.0\\times 0.6)}{2000}\\times 100\\% = \\frac{10(2.4 + 2.4)}{2000}\\times 100\\% = \\frac{48}{2000}\\times 100\\% = \\mathbf{2.40\\%}$$\n\n---\n\n### Problem 6 (DC Generator EMF Equation)\nA 4-pole DC generator with wave-wound armature ($A=2$) has $51$ slots with $20$ conductors per slot ($Z = 1020$), runs at $1500\\text{ RPM}$, and has flux per pole $\\Phi = 7\\text{ mWb}$. Find generated EMF $E_g$.\n\n**Solution:**\n$$E_g = \\frac{\\Phi Z N}{60}\\cdot\\frac{P}{A} = \\frac{(7\\times 10^{-3}) \\times 1020 \\times 1500}{60} \\times \\frac{4}{2} = 178.5 \\times 2 = \\mathbf{357.0\\text{ V}}$$\n\n---\n\n### Problem 7 (DC Shunt Motor Speed Calculation)\nA 6-pole lap-wound ($A=P=6$) DC shunt motor has $500$ armature conductors, $R_a = 0.05\\ \\Omega$, shunt field resistance $R_{sh} = 25\\ \\Omega$. It takes $I_L = 120\\text{ A}$ from $V = 100\\text{ V}$ DC mains with flux $\\Phi = 0.02\\text{ Wb}$. Find its speed $N$.\n\n**Solution:**\n1. Shunt field current $I_{sh} = \\frac{V}{R_{sh}} = \\frac{100}{25} = 4\\text{ A}$.\n2. Armature current $I_a = I_L - I_{sh} = 120 - 4 = \\mathbf{116\\text{ A}}$.\n3. Back EMF $E_b = V - I_a R_a = 100 - (116 \\times 0.05) = 100 - 5.8 = \\mathbf{94.2\\text{ V}}$.\n4. From $E_b = \\frac{\\Phi Z N P}{60 A}$ ($P/A = 6/6 = 1$):\n   $$N = \\frac{60 E_b}{\\Phi Z} = \\frac{60 \\times 94.2}{0.02 \\times 500} = \\frac{5652}{10} = \\mathbf{565.2\\text{ RPM}}$$\n\n---\n\n### Problem 8 (3-Phase Induction Motor Slip & Rotor Frequency)\nA 6-pole, $50\\text{ Hz}$ three-phase induction motor runs at $960\\text{ RPM}$ at full load. Calculate synchronous speed $N_s$, slip $s$, and rotor current frequency $f_r$.\n\n**Solution:**\n1. $N_s = \\frac{120 f}{P} = \\frac{120 \\times 50}{6} = \\mathbf{1000\\text{ RPM}}$.\n2. $s = \\frac{N_s - N_r}{N_s} = \\frac{1000 - 960}{1000} = 0.04 = \\mathbf{4.0\\%}$.\n3. $f_r = s f = 0.04 \\times 50 = \\mathbf{2.0\\text{ Hz}}$."
