          "title": "Solved Exam Numericals (Core BEEE High-Yield Problems)",
          "markdown": "## 8. Solved Exam Numericals (Core BEEE High-Yield Problems)\n\n### Problem 1 (Extrinsic Silicon Carrier Concentration & Conductivity)\n**Given:** Pure Silicon ($n_i = 1.5 \\times 10^{10}\\text{ cm}^{-3}$, $\\mu_n = 1350\\text{ cm}^2/\\text{V}\\cdot\\text{s}$) is doped with $N_D = 10^{17}\\text{ cm}^{-3}$. Find hole concentration $p$ and conductivity $\\sigma_n$ ($q = 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}\\text{ C}$).\n\n**Solution:**\n1. $n \\approx N_D = 10^{17}\\text{ cm}^{-3}$. By Mass-Action Law: $p = \\frac{n_i^2}{n} = \\frac{(1.5 \\times 10^{10})^2}{10^{17}} = \\mathbf{2.25 \\times 10^3\\text{ cm}^{-3}}$.\n2. $\\sigma_n = q n \\mu_n = (1.6 \\times 10^{-19})(10^{17})(1350) = \\mathbf{21.6\\text{ S/cm}}$.\n\n### Problem 2 (P-N Junction Diode Forward Current)\n**Given:** A Silicon diode ($V_\\gamma = 0.7\\text{ V}$) in series with a $10\\text{ k}\\Omega$ resistor connects across a $6\\text{ V}$ forward DC supply. Find diode current $I_D$.\n\n**Solution:**\n$$I_D = \\frac{V_S - V_\\gamma}{R} = \\frac{6 - 0.7}{10 \\times 10^3} = \\frac{5.3}{10\\,000} = \\mathbf{0.53\\text{ mA}}$$\n\n### Problem 3 (HWR vs Bridge FWR Comparison on 230 V / 23 V Transformer)\n**Given:** An ideal $230\\text{ V} / 23\\text{ V}$ RMS, $50\\text{ Hz}$ step-down transformer feeds $R_L = 500\\ \\Omega$. Compare $V_m$, $V_{dc}$, PIV, and ripple frequency for HWR vs Bridge FWR.\n\n**Solution:**\n1. $V_m = \\sqrt{2} \\times 23 = \\mathbf{32.53\\text{ V}}$.\n2. **HWR:** $V_{dc} = \\frac{V_m}{\\pi} = \\mathbf{10.35\\text{ V}}$, $\\text{PIV} = 32.53\\text{ V}$, $f_{\\text{rip}} = 50\\text{ Hz}$.\n3. **Bridge FWR:** $V_{dc} = \\frac{2V_m}{\\pi} = \\mathbf{20.71\\text{ V}}$, $\\text{PIV} = 32.53\\text{ V}$ (Centre-Tap FWR is $65.05\\text{ V}$), $f_{\\text{rip}} = 100\\text{ Hz}$.\n\n### Problem 4 (Zener Voltage Regulator Bounds)\n**Given:** A Zener diode ($V_Z = 10\\text{ V}$, $I_{Z,\\min} = 5\\text{ mA}$) regulates $R_L = 1\\text{ k}\\Omega$ from $V_{in} = 18\\text{ V}$. Find (a) $I_L$, (b) maximum series resistor $R_{s,\\max}$, and (c) worst-case Zener power $P_{Z,\\max}$ if $R_L$ opens.\n\n**Solution:**\n1. $I_L = \\frac{V_Z}{R_L} = \\frac{10}{1000} = \\mathbf{10\\text{ mA}}$.\n2. $I_{s,\\min} = I_{Z,\\min} + I_L = 5 + 10 = 15\\text{ mA} \\implies R_{s,\\max} = \\frac{18 - 10}{15 \\times 10^{-3}} = \\mathbf{533.33\\ \\Omega}$.\n3. At open load ($I_L = 0$), all $15\\text{ mA}$ flows through the Zener: $P_{Z,\\max} = V_Z I_s = 10 \\times 15\\text{ mA} = \\mathbf{150\\text{ mW}}$.\n\n### Problem 5 (Fixed-Bias NPN BJT Q-Point)\n**Given:** An NPN Silicon BJT ($\\beta = 100$, $V_{BE} = 0.7\\text{ V}$) has $V_{CC} = 12\\text{ V}$, $R_B = 220\\text{ k}\\Omega$, and $R_C = 2.2\\text{ k}\\Omega$. Find $I_B$, $I_C$, $V_{CE}$, and $\\alpha$.\n\n**Solution:**\n1. $I_B = \\frac{12 - 0.7}{220 \\times 10^3} = \\mathbf{51.36\\ \\mu\\text{A}}$\n2. $I_C = \\beta I_B = 100 \\times 51.364\\ \\mu\\text{A} = \\mathbf{5.136\\text{ mA}}$\n3. $V_{CE} = V_{CC} - I_C R_C = 12 - (5.1364 \\times 10^{-3})(2200) = \\mathbf{0.70\\text{ V}}$, and $\\alpha = \\frac{100}{101} = \\mathbf{0.9901}$.\n\n### Problem 6 (2's Complement Subtraction & Boolean Simplification)\n**Given:** (a) Subtract $(1010)_2$ from $(111)_2$ using 5-bit 2's complement. (b) Simplify $Y = XY + X\\overline{Y}Z + Y\\overline{Z}$.\n\n**Solution:**\n**(a)** $A = 00111_2$ ($+7$), $B = 01010_2$ ($+10$). 2's comp of $B = 10101 + 1 = 10110_2$.\n$$00111 + 10110 = 11101_2 \\quad (\\text{carry-out } = 0 \\implies \\text{negative})$$\nMagnitude $= \\text{2's comp}(11101_2) = 00011_2 = 3 \\implies \\mathbf{-3_{10}}$ ($-(00011)_2$).\n\n**(b)** Factor $X$: $Y = X(Y + \\overline{Y}Z) + Y\\overline{Z}$. Apply Absorption ($Y + \\overline{Y}Z = Y + Z$):\n$$Y = X(Y + Z) + Y\\overline{Z} = \\mathbf{XY + XZ + Y\\overline{Z}}$$"
